        <h2 id="weak-point" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">The Garage Entry Door Is the Real Weak Point</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          In many homes, the overhead door isn't the easiest way in. The door between the garage and the house often is. Once someone is inside a closed garage, that one entry door is all that stands between them and your living space, and it often gets the least attention.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Think about how a new house gets built. In newer builds, the garage-to-house door can be a basic unit with a builder-grade deadbolt, and nobody revisits it after move-in. Homeowners rekey the front door, then forget this one. We covered that gap in our <a href="/blog/new-home-smyrna-de-locksmith-checklist/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">Smyrna move-in locksmith checklist</a>, where the garage-to-house door is on the list of doors people skip.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Home age doesn't tell you how exposed your opener is. A new house can still have an older-style unit, and an older house may have been upgraded. Code type is what matters, and we cover it below. The big door and its remote get plenty of attention from security articles, but the interior door, the side service door, and the emergency release deserve the same care. We treat them as one system.
        </p>

        <h2 id="five-point-check" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What Is the 5-Point Garage Entry Check?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The 5-Point Garage Entry Check is a short walk-through of every way into your garage and from your garage into your house. We built it so you can run it in about fifteen minutes with a screwdriver and a flashlight. Each point catches a different weak spot.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-0"><strong class="text-[#1A1F22]">What is the 5-Point Garage Entry Check?</strong> It is a five-step review of the interior entry door, the emergency release, the opener's age and code type, the remotes and keypad, and the side door and windows. Together they cover the common ways into a garage.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Interior entry door.</strong> Is it solid-core or steel, not hollow? Does it have a keyed deadbolt? Pull one strike plate screw: if it is short and never reaches the wall framing, a hard kick can split the jamb.</li>
          <li><strong>Emergency release.</strong> Can someone outside hook the cord or handle? Is it shielded or lightly secured, yet still easy to pull from inside?</li>
          <li><strong>Opener age and code type.</strong> Check the label on the motor unit.</li>
          <li><strong>Remotes and keypad.</strong> Where do the remotes live? Who knows the keypad code? Are there old remotes nobody can find?</li>
          <li><strong>Side service door and windows.</strong> What lock does the side door use? Can someone see in through the glass? Is the area lit at night?</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          You'll spot most problems at points 1 and 2. Points 3 and 4 are about habits and old equipment. Point 5 is the one people forget until a neighbor mentions it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="lock-entry-door" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">How Do You Lock the Door From the Garage Into Your House?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Use a keyed deadbolt on a solid-core or steel door, reinforce the strike plate with long screws, and key the lock to match your other exterior doors. Treat this door like a front door, because the overhead door isn't meant to be your last line.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Here is what we'd check on your interior entry door, in order:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>The door itself.</strong> Knock on it. A hollow sound means a hollow door. Solid-core wood or steel resists force far better.</li>
          <li><strong>The deadbolt.</strong> A deadbolt that throws a solid bolt into the frame beats a spring latch alone. If yours is a basic knob lock with no deadbolt, that's a reason to book a <a href="/services/deadbolt-installation/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">deadbolt installation</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>The strike plate.</strong> Swap the short screws for 3-inch screws that bite into the wall stud. It takes a few minutes and a drill.</li>
          <li><strong>The key.</strong> If the lock isn't keyed like your front door, you're carrying an extra key. A <a href="/services/lock-rekeying/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">lock rekeying service</a> can match it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A second point on keys: many people leave the garage-to-house door unlocked because they use it all day. That's understandable. Try a keypad or smart deadbolt so the door locks behind you without a key hunt. If you want help picking a lock style for the front, our guide to the <a href="/blog/best-locks-for-your-front-door/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">best locks for your front door</a> covers the options.
        </p>

        <h3 id="side-door" class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">What about a garage side door lock?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The same rules apply. A side service door that opens straight into the garage should have a deadbolt, a reinforced strike, and ideally no big window beside the handle. Privacy film blocks sight lines into the garage, but it won't stop someone who breaks the glass and reaches the thumb-turn. If glass sits within arm's reach of the thumb-turn, the safer fix is a solid door panel or a glass-break-resistant security film. Skip any lock that needs a key to open from inside, because an exit door generally has to open quickly in an emergency. A locksmith can walk you through options that meet local egress and fire code.
        </p>

        <h2 id="release-cord" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Coat Hangers and Release Cords Are Only a Risk on Some Setups</h2>
        <img
          src="/images/garage-emergency-release-shield-smyrna-de.webp"
          alt="Garage door emergency release handle covered by a clear dome-style shield on the opener rail of a Smyrna, DE garage"
          width="1000"
          height="746"
          class="md:float-right w-full md:w-1/2 md:max-w-[360px] h-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 md:ml-6 mb-4"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          On some older openers, a coat hanger can work. If the emergency release cord hangs near a gap at the top of the door, a bent wire can sometimes hook it from outside and pull it. How exposed you are depends on how your release is positioned and whether it is shielded or removed from easy reach, so it is worth a look at yours.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          You have two practical fixes.
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>A dome-style release shield.</strong> It covers the handle so a hook can't catch it, and you can still pull it from inside.</li>
          <li><strong>A light zip tie.</strong> A thin tie blocks a weak outside pull. A strong inside pull snaps it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Now the safety part, and we mean it. The release exists so people can get out when the power fails or the opener jams. A permanent fastener, a thick tie, or wire can leave you stuck with a door that won't open during a power outage or an opener failure, which is exactly when the release is meant to work, as <a href="https://support.chamberlaingroup.com/s/article/How-to-Manually-Open-or-Close-a-Garage-Door" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chamberlain&#39;s support guidance</a> explains. If you use a tie, make it a light one you can break by hand from inside. Never disable the release for good.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Our advice: if your release is exposed and your opener is old, a shield beats a tie. A tie is a decent stopgap this afternoon.
        </p>
        <div class="clear-both"></div>

        <h2 id="rolling-code" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Are Rolling-Code Openers Safe From Code Grabbers?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Modern rolling-code openers resist simple replay attacks, while older fixed-code units do not. A rolling-code remote sends a different code each time, so a copied signal is useless on the next press. A fixed-code remote sends the same code every time.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          You'll see scary stories about pocket-sized radio gadgets and "code grabbers." Code type is the best predictor of exposure, and age is a useful clue to it. Here's a general guide, not a ranking of products:
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border border-gray-200">
            <thead class="bg-[#1A1F22] text-white">
              <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Opener type</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Typical exposure</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">What to do</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-700">
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Older fixed-code unit</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Same code every press, so a copied signal can replay</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Replace the opener or its receiver; treat the garage as unlocked until then</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Early rolling code</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Harder to copy, but older designs may have known weak points</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Check the label for age; consider an upgrade if it's well over a decade old</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Current rolling code</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Resists simple replay; the release still needs its own protection</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Keep remotes out of cars; reset memory if one goes missing</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Smart opener</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Adds phone control and activity logs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Use a strong account password; remove old users and remotes</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Not sure which one you have? Look at the label on the motor unit, or the model number, and search it. If you can't tell, a locksmith or garage door tech can read it in seconds.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          One habit matters for every type. If a remote is lost or stolen, reset the opener's memory so it ignores all old remotes, then reprogram the ones you still use. Your manual shows the steps, and it usually takes a few minutes.
        </p>

        <h2 id="habits" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Low-Effort Habits Make a Real Difference</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The easiest wins are habits, not hardware. Keep the overhead door closed when you're not using it, keep remotes on a keychain instead of the car visor, and add light near the garage and driveway. <a href="https://dsp.delaware.gov/2016/09/28/state-police-probe-residential-burglaries/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Delaware State Police burglary-prevention guidance</a> says to keep your garage door closed and locked, and to leave lights on or use timers when you are out.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Armed Forces Insurance (AFI) makes a similar point in its <a href="https://www.afi.org/Old-Resources-and-Tools/Homeowner-Insurance/Top-10-Garage-Door-Security-Tips-to-Prevent-Break-Ins" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">garage door security tips</a>: a thief who breaks into your car and takes the remote has a way into your home, and the door between the garage and house deserves a deadbolt. Both are easy to act on this week:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Remote on your keychain.</strong> A car that's broken into then hands over only a car key, not a garage opener.</li>
          <li><strong>Clear old codes and keys.</strong> If a contractor, former tenant, or builder ever had your keypad code or a key, change the code and <a href="/services/lock-rekeying/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">rekey the locks</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Close the door.</strong> A quick glance before bed beats an open garage all night.</li>
          <li><strong>Add light.</strong> A motion-sensor fixture over the driveway or side door takes little effort and tells a visitor they're seen.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          You may also run across slide locks and floor-mounted door defenders. They can help on track-style doors that rely on a manual lock. Disengage or unplug the opener (or put it in its lock or vacation mode) before you set any manual lock, and never run the opener with a slide lock engaged, because that can damage the door or the opener. On a typical sectional door with an opener they're a secondary step, so we'd handle the five points first.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Want an easy test? Stand in the driveway at night and look at the garage like a stranger would. What can you see through the windows? Where is it dark? Fix the first thing that bothers you.
        </p>

        <h2 id="call-locksmith" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">When Should You Call a Locksmith?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Call a locksmith for the work that depends on the lock itself: the interior entry door deadbolt, rekeying the garage entry lock, side door hardware, and keypad or smart lock installs. You can handle shields, remotes, and lighting on your own. Hardware that fails under force is worth doing right once.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Before you book anyone, ask what the visit covers. A trustworthy company confirms the scope of work in writing or by text before a technician is sent, and explains any change before touching a tool. That's how we work, and it's a fair standard for any company you call.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If you're in Smyrna or elsewhere in northern Kent County, we cover the area as part of our <a href="/service-areas/smyrna-de/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">Smyrna, DE service area</a>. Our <a href="/services/residential-locksmith/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">residential locksmith services</a> include deadbolts, rekeying, and lock repair, and you can <a href="/contact/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">request a garage entry door check</a> to describe what you've got. A photo of the entry door and strike plate is enough to start.
        </p>

        <h2 id="where-to-start" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Where Should You Start Securing a Garage Door?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Knowing how to secure a garage door comes down to doing a few things in the right order. The first step is the door between your garage and your house: confirm it's solid, deadbolted, and keyed like your front door. After that, the release, the remotes, and the lighting are quick fixes you can finish in a weekend.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 mt-10">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-2">Sources</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <li><a href="https://dsp.delaware.gov/2016/09/28/state-police-probe-residential-burglaries/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Delaware State Police, State Police Probe Residential Burglaries (Sept. 28, 2016)</a></li>
            <li><a href="https://support.chamberlaingroup.com/s/article/How-to-Manually-Open-or-Close-a-Garage-Door" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chamberlain Group Support, How to Manually Open or Close a Garage Door</a></li>
            <li><a href="https://www.afi.org/Old-Resources-and-Tools/Homeowner-Insurance/Top-10-Garage-Door-Security-Tips-to-Prevent-Break-Ins" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Armed Forces Insurance, Top 10 Garage Door Security Tips to Prevent Break-Ins</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 not-prose">
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              How do I make my garage door more secure?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Lock and reinforce the door from the garage into the house first. Then shield the emergency release, keep remotes out of your car, and check your opener's age and code type. Those four steps close the most common gaps without any special tools.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Can someone break into a garage with a coat hanger?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">It's possible on some older openers that have an exposed release cord and a gap at the top of the door. A dome shield or a light zip tie closes that gap. Check how your release is positioned and whether it is easy to reach.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Should I lock the door from the garage into the house?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Yes. Use a keyed deadbolt on a solid-core or steel door, and key it to match your other exterior locks. Reinforce the strike plate with 3-inch screws that reach the wall framing. Treat it like a front door, because it is the last barrier between a closed garage and your living space.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Can thieves copy my garage door opener code?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Older fixed-code systems are exposed, because the same code goes out on every press and a copied signal can be replayed. Modern rolling-code openers resist simple copying by changing the code each time. Check the label on your motor unit, or search its model number, to see which type you have.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Is it safe to zip-tie the emergency release?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Only with a light tie that snaps by hand from inside. A thick tie or wire can keep the release from working during a power outage or an opener failure. A dome-style shield is the safer, longer-lasting option because it never blocks the pull.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              What is the most secure way to lock a garage door from inside?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Use your opener's lock or vacation mode if it has one. If your door type allows a manual slide lock, disengage or unplug the opener before setting it, and never run the opener with a slide lock engaged. Pair that with a shielded release and a locked interior entry door.</p>
          </details>
        </div>

